REBOOT(8)            UNIX Programmer's Manual            REBOOT(8)



NAME
     reboot, halt - stopping and restarting the system

SYNOPSIS
     /sbin/reboot [ -lqnhdarsfRD ]
     /sbin/halt [ -lqndars ]

DESCRIPTION
     Reboot syncs the disks, marks every writable file system clean,
     and cold-starts the machine.  Halt is the same binary under
     another name: it runs the same shutdown and then stops the
     processor instead of restarting it, which is what "reboot -h"
     does.

     The shutdown is upstream's.  Reboot logs what is about to
     happen, syncs, sends init(8) a SIGTSTP so it stops respawning
     lines, then SIGTERM to every process to let each one exit
     cleanly.  What is still alive after that gets SIGKILL, in
     rounds, until nothing is left.  Only then does it call
     reboot(2).

     Options are:

     -l   Don't log what is about to happen.  Without it the line
          goes to syslogd(8), and /etc/syslog.conf sends it to
          /usr/adm/messages and the console.

     -q   Reboot quickly and ungracefully, without shutting down
          running processes first.

     -n   Don't sync before rebooting.  This can be used if a disk
          or the processor is on fire.

     -h   Don't reboot, simply halt the processor.

     -d   Dump memory onto the dump device before rebooting.

     -a   Have the system booter ask for the name of the system to
          be booted, rather than immediately booting the default.

     -r   Mount the root file system read only when the system
          reboots.

     -s   Don't enter multi-user mode after the system has rebooted
          - stay in single user mode.

     -f   Fast reboot.  Omit the automatic file system consistency
          check when the system reboots.

     -R   Tell the kernel to use the compiled in root device rather
          than the one it was booted from.

     -D   Set the autoconfig(8) debug flag.

     THE KERNEL READS TWO OF THEM.  reboot(2) acts on -n and -h; the
     rest are parsed, collected for the log line and then ignored,
     because the machinery each one names does not exist here.  A
     IIgs has no dump device, the boot program takes no system name
     and no flags from a rebooting kernel, there is no
     single-user mode and no autoconfig(8), and the root is chosen
     by the boot program rather than by a compiled in device.  The
     boot fsck is skipped on a file system the kernel marked clean,
     which is what -f asks for and gets without asking.

     A restart is a cold start through the Apple IIgs firmware's
     PWRUP entry, which reinitialises the hardware and scans for a
     startup device.  The screen is repainted by the firmware, so
     the banner reboot(2) prints is the last thing this system puts
     there.

     /etc/rc runs reboot when fsck(8) exits 4, which is fsck saying
     it repaired the root and the system must restart before
     anything reads it again.

FILES
     /usr/adm/wtmp          shutdown record

SEE ALSO
     fsck(8), init(8), rc(8), sync(1), sync(2), syslog(3)

BUGS
     Reboot writes its shutdown record by appending to
     /usr/adm/wtmp if that file exists.  /usr/adm does exist
     now -- syslog(3)'s socketless fallback appends to
     /usr/adm/messages -- but no wtmp is kept there, so no
     record is written and nothing reports its absence.

     Reboot(2) enforces no privilege check, so any process can stop
     the machine.  Upstream's is "if (suser()) boot(rootdev,
     uap->howto)", and this waits on the credentials the rest of the
     system is waiting on.



